{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 0,
    "dDurationMs": 2760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "People love to imagine that the things \nthey don’t understand.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2760,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are somehow connected to each other. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5880,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, quantum mechanics and consciousness, or aliens and pyramids, or black holes and dark matter,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11400,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or dark matter and dark energy, dark energy \nand black holes. Usually there’s no real  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16320,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relationship whatsoever, but this last pair—black \nholes and dark energy being the same thing—has  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21960,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "received some recent hype in the press. \nLet’s see if it might actually be true."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33780,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here’s the idea in a nutshell. Black holes are apparently made of dark energy. As the universe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40200,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expands, black holes in that expanding space \ngain mass proportional to the increasing volume  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44580,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the universe. Which results in two things: one, black holes should be growing faster than we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50760,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expect based only on what they eat. And \ntwo: black holes could actually be the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56580,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cause of the accelerating expansion of the \nuniverse. This is the claim in an article  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61560,
    "dDurationMs": 7380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by University of Hawaii astrophysicists \nDuncan Farrah and Kevin Croker and their team."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68940,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now to really assess whether we should dismiss this or take note, we have to follow the paper trail back.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75960,
    "dDurationMs": 6900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Way, way back. The seed of this idea came from a \nlittle paper from the mid 60s by Russian physicist  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82860,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Erast Gliner. someone you’ve probably never heard. In fact not many people have heard of Gliner,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88620,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but this work led to an alternative way \nof thinking about the nature of nothing,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94500,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and set the stage for our theories of \nboth cosmic inflation and dark energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100320,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So a little science history—stuff we’ve covered before if you’re interested in more detail.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106440,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Soon after Einstein first came up with his general \ntheory of relativity, another Russian physicist  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112260,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Alexander Friedman solved its equations for \nthe entire universe. The resulting Friedman  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118320,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equations predicted that the universe must be \nexpanding or contracting—which Einstein famously  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123900,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "disliked, and so he added something called the \ncosmological constant to general relativity so  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130680,
    "dDurationMs": 7740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the universe described by Friedman's \nsolution had the potential to be static.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138420,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The universe was of course subsequently \nshown to be expanding, so the idea of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142980,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the cosmological constant was \nlargely ignored for years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146040,
    "dDurationMs": 8520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But Erast Gliner realised that the cosmological \nconstant could be interpreted as an energy in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154560,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fabric of space itself—a vacuum energy—and that this stuff would have bizarre properties. For example  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161280,
    "dDurationMs": 7620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "possessing negative pressure. Gliner’s energetic vacuum became a fundamental part of the science  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168900,
    "dDurationMs": 7140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of cosmology. It’s the mathematical mechanism behind cosmic inflation—the extreme expansion rate that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176040,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "many cosmologists believe kickstarted \nthe Big Bang—and behind dark energy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181560,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the mysterious influence that’s currently causing \nthe expansion of the universe to accelerate.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186840,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which, by the way, tells us that the \ncosmological constant is in some sense real,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191520,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even if Einstein added it to his \nequations for the wrong reasons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195960,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gliner’s 1966 paper changed cosmology with \nthis new way of thinking about the cosmological  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202080,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "constant. But there was another rather minor \nnote in that paper. One that sent a handful  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209100,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of scientists down the unusual train of thought leading to the result we are discussing today.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215340,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gliner speculated that his energetic vacuum \ncould be an endpoint of gravitational collapse.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222120,
    "dDurationMs": 7620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If so, perhaps this stuff also \ndescribes the interior of black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 229740,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, in regular general relativity, a black hole occurs when an object reaches such a high density that an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236580,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "event horizon forms around it—a surface below \nwhich escape from the object’s gravitational  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241800,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field is impossible. In standard GR, matter that \ncollapses into a black hole ends up crushed into  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248820,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an infinitesimal point of infinite density—the \nsingularity at the black hole’s center. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254400,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the prediction of the singularity in general \nrelativity is slightly awkward, because it brings  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260160,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the theory into hopeless conflict with quantum \nmechanics, for reasons we’ve discussed before.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266040,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many people have tried to \nfind ways around this issue,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268680,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for example with wormholes or \nthe fuzzballs of string theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 272400,
    "dDurationMs": 3780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gliner’s insight gives another way to rescue  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276180,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collapsing matter from breaking \nthe laws of quantum mechanics.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280200,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If that matter can transmute to a raw energy \nof the vacuum then the resulting negative  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 286800,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pressure can potentially counteract gravitational \ncollapse, preventing singularity. A number of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 292500,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "versions of this idea have been proposed over \nthe years. For example we have the dark-energy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297360,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "star of George Chapline and Robert Laughlin, or \nthe gravastar of Pawel Mazur and Emil Mottola.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305760,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The general name for these things is the \nGEODE - generic object of dark energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 311280,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "GEODEs function as black holes in many \nways—including being compact, intense  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 317340,
    "dDurationMs": 7740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sources of gravity that are almost completely \ninvisible. They also differ in subtle ways that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 325080,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "may one day be detectable. For example, there may \nbe signatures in the gravitational waves they emit  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330600,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when they merge. But currently we don’t have any \ndirect observational evidence that black holes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335820,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are anything other than the things predicted by \ngeneral relativity. That evidence is hard to come  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341520,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by because the laws of physics make it literally \nimpossible to see beneath the event horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 346260,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if black holes are GEODEs there may \nbe a sneaky way to reveal their nature.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353280,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the aspect of the new work \nthat’s the most controversial.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358680,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Regular black holes are fundamentally isolated. \nOutside, we can only sense their gravitational  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 364740,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field and electric charge. A black hole \nonly knows about what falls into it. It  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370260,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "certainly doesn’t know what the universe \nis doing very far from its event horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 374820,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But according to the scientists behind this new \nwork, a GEODE is connected to the universe on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 380940,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the largest scales. In a 2021 paper led by Kevin \nCroker, they find that a new way to look  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 386700,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at the Friedman equations they claim demonstrate something very surprising—compact regions of dark energy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 393300,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are coupled to the expansion of the entire \ncosmos. They call this cosmological coupling,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399360,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and they claim that as the universe grows, \nso does a GEODE—that their mass should be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 405180,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "proportional to the volume of the universe. OK, \nso if that’s true, then “black holes” —we’ll  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411420,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just call the GEODEs black holes from now on—should have grown enormously since the early universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417180,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that’s the assertion of the new paper \nby Farrah and collaborators. In particular,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422400,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they claim that the supermassive black holes \nat the centers of galaxies in the modern  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 427140,
    "dDurationMs": 7058,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe are way too big. All galaxies have \nthese “SMBHs”. They probably began as medium  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 434198,
    "dDurationMs": 3922,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sized black holes from the gigantic first \ngeneration of stars in the early universe,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 438120,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and grew by merging with other black \nholes and devouring or “accreting” gas  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444360,
    "dDurationMs": 3780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "until they reach millions and even \nbillions of times the Sun’s mass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448140,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The team claims that their sample \nof SMBHs grew too much over the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454440,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "last 10 billion years or so to be \nexplained by accretion or mergers alone.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 459780,
    "dDurationMs": 7260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "More precisely, they claim that galaxies now \nhave SMBHs that are much bigger relative to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 467040,
    "dDurationMs": 8280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their mass in stars than galaxies in the past. \nIn general, bigger galaxies have bigger SMBHs.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475320,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this team claims that the ratio between \nblack hole mass and galaxy mass has changed,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480840,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with the black holes outpacing the growth \nof galaxies. And that this extra growth is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486060,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exactly the amount expected from “cosmological \ncoupling” — so due to being connected to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492360,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expansion of the universe. They claim 99.98% \nconfidence in excluding “zero coupling”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 499800,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In a separate paper from 2021 the same team also \npoints out that the merging black holes detected  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 505200,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by LIGO are also way too big — and again, by about \nthe amount expected due to cosmological coupling."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 511980,
    "dDurationMs": 8340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It all sounds pretty exciting, right? But wait, \nthat’s not all. The team also claims that these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 520320,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black holes might not just be made of dark energy \n- they might literally be dark energy. As in,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 526560,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's the dark energy in black holes that’s \ncausing the accelerating expansion of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 532140,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe, rather than the energy \nof the vacuum between the galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 536760,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s see how that’s supposed to work. The key \nto the exponentially accelerating power of dark  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 542820,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy is simply its constant energy density. \nAs the universe expands, regular matter and dark  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549180,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter thin out and their gravitational influence \ndiminishes. But dark energy is usually described  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 555600,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as a property of the fabric of space itself, and \nso the more space you have the more dark energy.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 562260,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Expanding space actually adds energy into the \nsystem. For complicated reasons due to relativity  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568320,
    "dDurationMs": 6900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "being weird, this causes accelerating expansion. \nCheck these episodes for the gory details."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 575220,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so if dark energy isn’t in space \nitself, but is in the black holes,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580680,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "AND if the black holes are growing in mass with \nthe expanding universe, then you get the same  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585540,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "effect. Although the black holes get further apart \nas space expands, their masses increase, so their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 591240,
    "dDurationMs": 6305,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass density and their energy density remains constant—just like regular dark energy.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 597545,
    "dDurationMs": 4675,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For that reason they should behave like regular \ndark energy and cause accelerating expansion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 602220,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So could these GEODE black holes be numerous \nenough to account for dark energy? That’s a tall  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608280,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "order, because dark energy makes up around 70% \nof the energy of the universe. On the other hand,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613980,
    "dDurationMs": 6900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "regular matter makes up around 5%, and regular \nmatter is where black holes supposedly come from,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 620880,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "via the deaths of massive stars. But there’s a \nway around this. If you make black holes with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 627000,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "regular matter, and those black holes grow with \ncosmic expansion, then they can exceed that 5%."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 633600,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Anyway, Farrah and team calculate \nthe mass in black holes of all types  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 639300,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that should have been created by dying \nstars over the history of the universe,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 643200,
    "dDurationMs": 5161,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then calculate how these should have \ngrown due to cosmological coupling.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648361,
    "dDurationMs": 5039,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They find that it’s not that hard to \nget the amount needed for dark energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653400,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So that’s the idea in a nutshell. That black \nhole interiors contain an energetic vacuum which  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 660360,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is coupled to the expansion of the universe. That \nmeans black holes grow in mass with the expanding  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665400,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe, which in turn causes them to act like \nregular dark energy and accelerate that expansion.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672000,
    "dDurationMs": 3780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now that we have that \nstraight, let’s tear it apart.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 675780,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we can. We need to address both \nthe theoretical side and the evidence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 682080,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the theoretical side, we have 1. the idea \nthat black hole interiors might be dark energy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 687420,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and then 2. that this dark energy might be \ncoupled to the universe on cosmic scales.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 693840,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For 1. There isn’t any evidence, nor \nis there a particularly compelling  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 698400,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physical mechanism—but it’s at least \nplausible because we don’t understand  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 702720,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black holes fully anyway. Let’s \njust give them that one for now."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 707460,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now the idea that this internal vacuum energy is coupled on cosmic scales feels like the biggest  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713160,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reach of this whole idea. Normally, we think of \ngeneral relativity as a purely local theory. That  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719340,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "means every point in spacetime only knows about \nwhat’s happening to its neighbouring points.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 724680,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means that the interior and exterior of a \nblack hole only interact via information that can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 730980,
    "dDurationMs": 6104,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be transmitted across the event horizon by stuff \nfalling in or by Hawking radiation leaking out.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 737084,
    "dDurationMs": 6256,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But these mechanisms do not allow black holes to \nbe influenced by the universe on cosmic scales.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 743340,
    "dDurationMs": 2700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Supermassive black holes are \nin the centers of galaxies.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 746040,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The immediate spacetime that these \nblack holes live in is NOT expanding.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 751800,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The expanding universe solution to the Einstein \nequations comes from assuming matter is perfectly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 757680,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "smoothly distributed through the universe. It’s an \napproximation which works on the largest scales."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763980,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Standard general relativity seems to deny \nthe possibility of cosmological coupling,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 769140,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "however these researchers claim that their \nreformulation of the Friedman equations allows  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 775920,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this weird communication between small and \nlarge scales. This is extremely speculative,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 781080,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and no one besides this team \nhas published anything that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784680,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "properly analyses this conjecture. On the \ntheoretical side, this is the flimsiest part.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790260,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, if black holes really are made of vacuum \nenergy AND this cosmological coupling is right,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 796020,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then the bit about black holes accounting for \ndark energy is at least theoretically plausible.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 800880,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Although it would require that almost all black \nholes are not in galaxies, but rather distributed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 808080,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "evenly through the universe, rather than being \nclustered like the stars that they came from.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813660,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The authors claim that this is possible, but \nwe don’t have time to pick this apart today."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 817980,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, the final thing to look at is the main claim \nof the most recent paper—the supposed evidence  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 823920,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that supermassive black holes have grown so much \nthat they must be coupled to cosmic expansion.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830400,
    "dDurationMs": 7140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For more analysis of this side of it check out \nDr. Becky’s excellent video on this. But here’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837540,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "my hot take. These guys are not the first to try \nto measure the growth of black holes over cosmic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 843180,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "history. We’ve known for many years that there’s \na tight relationship between black hole mass and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 848940,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "galaxy mass, and for many years people have also \nbeen trying to measure the evolution of this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 854220,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relationship over cosmic time. Some suggest \nthat black holes grew faster than galaxies,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 859560,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some suggest that galaxies grew faster \nthan their black holes. This team's new  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 864600,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "result showing rapid black hole growth does some things differently from previous work. But it’s not clear  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 870540,
    "dDurationMs": 7791,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that they did the analysis better than the many experts who have been doing this sort of calculation for decades.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 878331,
    "dDurationMs": 5349,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The fact is, the evolution of black hole \nmasses is really really hard to measure,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 883680,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "largely because those masses are hard to \nestimate. It’s also very difficult to know  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 889920,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the galaxies we’re looking at from an \nearlier universe, billions of light years away,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 895080,
    "dDurationMs": 7380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are representative precursors to the galaxies \nwe’re looking at in the nearby, modern universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 902460,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Remember we can't actually see a single black hole evolve."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 906660,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are many potential biases involved in a study like this, and that fact isn’t properly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 913200,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "acknowledged in this paper. In fact, their \nclaim of “excluding zero cosmological coupling”  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 919500,
    "dDurationMs": 8700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "99.98% feels dishonest in the absence of a full \ndiscussion of the contingencies of the result.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 928200,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The confidence with which the result \nis stated is a bit of a red flag."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 932640,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so that’s my take on the black \nholes equals dark energy idea. Lots  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 937920,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of interesting stuff in there, and it \nmight be true if the most speculative  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 942720,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "parts can be verified by other experts. As \nwith anything this potentially revolutionary,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 948840,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s probably not true. But that doesn’t \nmean it’s not valuable. In order to find  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 953880,
    "dDurationMs": 6258,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the next big breakthrough, we need to explore \nall sorts of weird parts of theory space.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 960138,
    "dDurationMs": 6462,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And following the intuitions of Erast Gliner has led to incredible discoveries in the past,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 966600,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so perhaps they will again. And perhaps \nblack holes turn out to be dark energy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 972000,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "neatly wrapping together two of the most \nperplexing phenomena in all of space time."
    } ]
  } ]
}
